---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import NewsletterCta from '../../components/NewsletterCta.astro';
import { withBase, fmtMed } from '../../lib/site';
import { getWeeklySorted } from '../../lib/content';
import { NEWSLETTER, NEWSLETTER_LIVE } from '../../lib/newsletter';

// The subscribe page. Also the honest disclosure page: what we store, what we
// send, who runs the machine, and how to leave. If a developer-marketing guide
// runs a shady list, nothing else it says survives.

const weekly = await getWeeklySorted();
const recent = weekly.slice(0, 6);
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Newsletter"
  description="The Week by email: one short issue every Monday on what moved in developer marketing, each claim sourced. Self-hosted, double opt-in, no tracking pixels, one-click unsubscribe."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <div class="kicker"><span class="prompt" aria-hidden="true">&gt;</span> newsletter</div>
    <h1 class="page-title">{NEWSLETTER.name}, in your inbox</h1>
    <p class="dek">
      {NEWSLETTER.promise} Sent {NEWSLETTER.cadence}, written by the same desk
      that writes <a href={withBase('/weekly')}>the digest</a>, and delivered by
      a mail sender that lives in this repository.
    </p>
  </div>

  <NewsletterCta
    variant="page"
    source="/newsletter"
    heading="Subscribe"
    blurb="Your address, and nothing else. You will get one confirmation email; confirm it and the next issue arrives on Monday."
  />

  <div class="fm-sec">
    <span class="amber">##</span><span class="fm-sec-l">what arrives</span>
    <span class="fm-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>
  <ul class="nlp-list">
    <li><b>One issue a week.</b> Monday morning, UTC. Never more than one, and nothing on the weeks where nothing happened.</li>
    <li><b>The same words as the site.</b> The email is the week's digest rendered for mail, not a teaser that makes you click through.</li>
    <li><b>Every claim linked.</b> If an issue says a number moved, the source is one tap away.</li>
    <li><b>Plain text alongside the HTML.</b> Your terminal mail client gets a readable copy, because some of you read mail in mutt and we know it.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="fm-sec">
    <span class="amber">##</span><span class="fm-sec-l">how the list is run</span>
    <span class="fm-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </div>
  <div class="nlp-prose">
    <p>
      There is no Mailchimp account behind this, no Substack, no beehiiv. The
      subscribe form posts to a small service we wrote, the list is an
      append-only file on a machine we control, and the issues are rendered and
      addressed by our own sender — all of it in
      <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing/tree/main/newsletter">
        <code>newsletter/</code></a>.
      The one outside part is the last one: a mail relay accepts each finished
      message and delivers it, because sending from our own machine would mean
      worse deliverability, not more independence. It never sees the list, only
      the message in front of it, and swapping it is one line of configuration.
    </p>
    <ul class="nlp-list">
      <li>
        <b>Double opt-in.</b> Subscribing sends one confirmation email. Ignore it
        and nothing further is ever sent — an unconfirmed address is dropped, not
        kept warm.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>What is stored.</b> Your address, the page you subscribed from, the
        timestamps, and a salted hash of the IP that submitted the form (kept for
        rate-limiting, never reversible to you). Nothing else. No name field, no
        company enrichment, no profile.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>No tracking.</b> No open pixel, no click-wrapped links, no per-reader
        identifiers in URLs. We cannot tell whether you opened an issue, which is
        the point. The only number we have is how many people are on the list.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>One-click unsubscribe.</b> Every issue carries a signed unsubscribe
        link and the <code>List-Unsubscribe</code> headers your mail client uses
        for its own unsubscribe button. One request removes you; no confirmation
        flow, no "are you sure", no exit survey.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Never shared.</b> The list is not sold, rented, swapped, uploaded as a
        custom audience, or used to look you up anywhere else. The relay that
        delivers the mail is handed one message at a time, never the list.
      </li>
    </ul>
    {!NEWSLETTER_LIVE && (
      <p class="nlp-note">
        <b>Note.</b> This build has no capture endpoint configured, so the form
        above is not live yet. The
        <a href={withBase('/feed.xml')}>RSS feed</a> carries every issue in the
        meantime, and
        <a href="https://github.com/albertogrande/developer-marketing/blob/main/newsletter/README.md">
          newsletter/README.md</a> documents how the endpoint gets switched on.
      </p>
    )}
  </div>

  {recent.length > 0 && (
    <Fragment>
      <div class="fm-sec">
        <span class="amber">##</span><span class="fm-sec-l">read it first</span>
        <span class="fm-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="dim">{String(weekly.length).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
      </div>
      <ul class="fm-list">
        {recent.map((w) => (
          <li class="fm-row">
            <span class="fm-ln">{w.data.week.slice(5)}</span>
            <div class="fm-rowmain">
              <a href={withBase(`/weekly/${w.id}`)} class="fm-row-t">{w.data.title}</a>
              <p class="fm-row-dek">{w.data.summary} <span class="dim">· {fmtMed(w.data.date)}</span></p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <p class="muted-line">
        <a href={withBase('/weekly')}>every issue →</a>
        <span class="sb-sep" aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
        <a href={withBase('/feed.xml')}>≋ feed</a>
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  )}
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .nlp-list { padding-left: 1.1rem; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .nlp-list li { margin: 0.45rem 0; font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.55; }
  .nlp-prose { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .nlp-prose p { font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.6; }
  .nlp-note {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
</style>
